# Line endings and per-type handling.
#
# Development happens on Windows, CI and every deployment target are Linux, and
# `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine setting nobody in a clone inherits. Pinning
# the rules here is what makes a checkout identical on both.

# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
# Not just `text=auto`: with autocrlf on, that still hands Windows a CRLF working
# tree, which is how a shell script or a Makefile acquires a `\r` that only fails
# on Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Byte-exact artifacts: never convert, never diff as text ----------------

# Go fuzz corpus entries are reproduction material. The payload is an escaped Go
# string literal, so the bytes matter exactly; a seed that git rewrote on
# checkout is a seed that no longer reproduces what it was recorded for. Go's
# parser currently tolerates a CRLF header line, which is luck, not a contract.
#
# This repository has a concrete stake in that: the corpus under
# FuzzDecodeFields carries byte 0xf9 — the HPACK Huffman symbol whose miscoding
# shipped in v0.4.3 on both HTTP/2 and HTTP/3.
**/testdata/fuzz/** -text

# --- Explicit where breakage is silent and platform-specific ---------------
#
# Covered by the `*` rule above; spelled out so the intent survives someone
# narrowing that rule, because each of these fails only on the other platform.

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.tpl       text eol=lf

# --- Diff quality ----------------------------------------------------------

# Built-in hunk-header driver: shows the enclosing func/type in `@@` lines.
*.go   diff=golang
*.md   diff=markdown

